  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">WLSA - Strengthening Civil Society Engagement and Participation in Zimbabwe</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The purpose of the network is to contribute to sustained well-being of women and girl children through action-oriented research in the socio-legal field and advocating for women&#x2019;s rights. WLSA work incorporates action into research by questioning and challenging the law, instigating campaigns for changes in laws, policies and plans of action, educating women and girls about their rights, providing legal advice and gender sensitizing communities and leadership.
The primary problem that we seek to address is the lack of constitutional awareness and literacy among women which has led to them having a poor sense of ownership of the law leading to lack of assertiveness. While the development of the constitution was progressive, the non-alignment of the laws and the piecemeal, selective implementation of the constitution has resulted to threats to the rule of law, undermining independent commissions, the violations of human rights and the non-entrenchment of constitutionalism. In the aforementioned areas, women are disproportionately affected by poor service delivery; inadequate access to health and education, increased gender-based violence and harmful cultural practices such as early marriages due to patriarchy. Furthermore, there is lack of sustainable use of natural resources, corruption and limited participation and representation of women in key decision-making processes which continue to disempower women. The limited enjoyment of constitutional provisions has led women to apathy due to an inability to engage with policy makers in demanding improved access to their rights. The project seeks to empower women to identify issues, self-organize and approach duty bearers to demand and defend their rights.</narrative>
  </description>
